<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="bank-payment" package="com.axelor.apps.bankpayment.db"/>

  <!-- Gives a bank statement a company of its own, independent of its lifecycle.

    Stock BankStatement has no company at all: the only route to one is through its lines'
    bank accounts (BankStatementLine.bankDetails.company), which exist only after the file has
    been imported. A RECEIVED statement therefore belongs, as far as the database is concerned,
    to nobody — so every company-scoped read had to answer "not found" for a statement that is
    plainly sitting on the Bank statements screen.

    Nullable on purpose, and filled in from exactly four places — nothing else writes it:
      * the form's onNew default (the active company), for statements created on the screen;
      * OcrBankStatementCreatorImpl, from the company of the AI-OCR upload it came from;
      * BankStatementImportRunService, after the imported lines have proved which account — and
        therefore which company — the file is for, and only when the field was empty;
      * a person choosing it on the form, which is the only route for a legacy RECEIVED record.

    There is deliberately no save-time backfill. Stamping a line-less legacy statement with the
    active company of whoever happens to open it attributes somebody's bank file on the strength
    of a click, and a wrong company here is worse than no company: unattributed is visible and
    correctable, wrong is invisible and believed. Legacy IMPORTED rows need no write at all — the
    read scope derives their company from their lines when the lines are unanimous (see
    BankStatementSearchService). -->
  <entity name="BankStatement">

    <many-to-one name="company" ref="com.axelor.apps.base.db.Company" title="Company"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
